Description: vaapi_encode: prefer QVBR when -qp and -maxrate are both set
 VAAPI hardware mutually excludes CQP (Constant QP) mode from rate
 caps. The driver either errors or silently ignores -maxrate/-bufsize
 in CQP mode — there is no CRF-like "quality target with bitrate
 ceiling" semantic in CQP. Plex Media Server, however, routinely
 emits both `-qp:N` and `-maxrate:N` on hevc_vaapi / h264_vaapi
 sessions: PMS expresses intent as "encode at constant quality up to
 a network-safe bitrate ceiling". Stock vaapi_encode.c sees the
 explicit -qp first, short-circuits to CQP, and the iHD driver
 silently drops the -maxrate. Live impact observed 2026-05-14 on a
 4K HEVC HDR session: PMS argv `-qp:0 15 -maxrate:0 20000k` produced
 a measured ~200 Mbps output, exceeding LG webOS's network buffer
 and causing playback hitches.
 .
 The correct VAAPI mode for "constant-quality target + bitrate
 ceiling" is QVBR (Intelligent Constant Quality, per the Intel iHD
 manual). QVBR is `quality=1, maxrate=1, bitrate=1, hrd=1` in the
 vaapi_encode_rc_modes table — it uses `rc_quality` (which binds
 from `ctx->explicit_qp` at the `rc_mode_found:` block) as the
 target quality, `avctx->rc_max_rate` as the bitrate ceiling, and
 `avctx->bit_rate` as the average target. Scene-adaptive: the
 encoder spends bits on complex frames up to the ceiling, lower on
 simple frames.
 .
 With this patch, when the user provides BOTH `-qp` and `-maxrate`
 AND has not explicitly chosen an `-rc_mode`, vaapi_encode auto-
 selects QVBR (falling through to VBR if the driver lacks QVBR
 support, e.g. older Intel iGPUs). The downstream rc_quality binding
 already routes `ctx->explicit_qp` through unchanged, so the quality
 intent survives the mode switch.
 .
 Override:
   * `-rc_mode cqp` — keep strict QP (historical behaviour;
     -maxrate silently ignored by driver)
   * `-rc_mode vbr` — pure rate-targeted VBR (loses quality intent;
     pre-patch scaleplex rewriter behaviour)
   * `-rc_mode cbr` — strict constant bitrate
 .
 If `-b:v` is unset (PMS's typical CQP-shape argv has no bit_rate),
 default it to `rc_max_rate` so QVBR's "bitrate must be set"
 invariant holds. The rc_quality binding ensures quality is the
 actual driver — the bitrate fields become a budget cap, not a
 content driver.
 .
 The scaleplex worker rewriter's CQP→VBR translation (rc:CQP(qp=N)
 ->VBR(b:v=M) tag) can now be retired entirely — PMS argv passes
 through to the encoder unchanged. The SCALEPLEX_RC_MODE env knob
 it carried for CBR/CQP overrides is also retired; users override
 at the argv level via `-rc_mode <mode>`.
Origin: scaleplex
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: scaleplex
Last-Update: 2026-05-14
---
--- a/libavcodec/vaapi_encode.c
+++ b/libavcodec/vaapi_encode.c
@@ -1255,6 +1255,29 @@
     if (ctx->explicit_rc_mode)
         TRY_RC_MODE(ctx->explicit_rc_mode, 1);

+    // scaleplex/PLEX: PMS argv frequently combines -qp:N (constant-
+    // quality intent) with -maxrate:M (network-safe bitrate ceiling)
+    // on hevc_vaapi / h264_vaapi. VAAPI hardware mutually excludes
+    // CQP from rate caps — the explicit_qp short-circuit below
+    // selects CQP, the iHD driver silently ignores -maxrate, and
+    // PMS qp=15 4K HEVC HDR sessions overshoot a 20 Mbps ceiling by
+    // ~10x (observed 2026-05-14, LG webOS network buffer underruns).
+    // When both flags are present and the user has NOT explicitly
+    // chosen an rc_mode, prefer QVBR (quality target + bitrate
+    // ceiling). Falls back to VBR if the driver lacks QVBR (older
+    // Intel iGPUs). The downstream rc_quality binding at
+    // rc_mode_found: already routes ctx->explicit_qp through, so
+    // quality intent survives the mode switch. Override with
+    // `-rc_mode cqp` to keep historical strict-QP behaviour.
+    if (ctx->explicit_qp && avctx->rc_max_rate > 0) {
+        av_log(avctx, AV_LOG_INFO, "scaleplex: -qp + -maxrate both "
+               "set; selecting QVBR (use -rc_mode cqp to override).\n");
+        if (avctx->bit_rate <= 0)
+            avctx->bit_rate = avctx->rc_max_rate;
+        TRY_RC_MODE(RC_MODE_QVBR, 0);
+        TRY_RC_MODE(RC_MODE_VBR, 0);
+    }
+
     if (ctx->explicit_qp)
         TRY_RC_MODE(RC_MODE_CQP, 1);

